(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["advayataraka"]={"slug":"advayataraka","name":"Advayatāraka","deva":"अद्वयतारक","note":"companion","intro":["The Advaya-tāraka Upaniṣad (advayatārakopaniṣad, “the Upaniṣad of the non-dual deliverer”) is a short prose Upaniṣad of nineteen mantras transmitted in the Śukla Yajurveda tradition. In the Muktikā canon of 108 Upaniṣads it is number 53; among the twenty Yoga Upaniṣads it is the principal text on tāraka-yoga — the yoga of “that which carries one across.” Its central subject is the direct contemplation of inner light (jyoti-darśana) as the means to liberation.","Where the Brahma-sūtras, the kārikās of Gauḍapāda, and the major Upaniṣads provide the conceptual architecture of advaita, the Advaya-tāraka provides the operative method by which the non-dual is realised: a specific set of contemplations on inner and outer light, supported by the mudrās śāmbhavī and khecarī, and culminating in the state called amanaska (“no-mind”)."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Advaya-Tāraka Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A Reference Companion","Word-by-Word Translation · Commentary Synthesis · Visual Schemata · Practical Sādhana","All 19 mantras · Three Lakṣyas · Śāmbhavī &amp; Khecarī · Tāraka-Yoga","Compiled 2026"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"A Note on Sources & Scope","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Read this first — The Advaya-tāraka is one of the twenty Yoga Upaniṣads of the Śukla Yajurveda. The text consists of 19 prose mantras (not metrical verses). This companion presents each mantra in Devanāgarī, IAST, and literal word-by-word — followed by a synthesis drawn principally from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Sanskrit Dīpikā (c. 1750) and corroborated against the standard modern editions cited at the end. Where editions vary in mantra-numbering or phrasing, this is noted at the relevant verse.</td></tr></table>","<table><tr><td>On “five commentaries” — A genuine fact: only ONE classical Sanskrit commentary on this Upaniṣad survives — Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā. The other four entries in Part VI are the most consequential modern critical editions / translations / expositions. This companion does not fabricate Sanskrit commentaries that do not exist.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1.1  What the Text Is","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Advaya-tāraka Upaniṣad (advayatārakopaniṣad, “the Upaniṣad of the non-dual deliverer”) is a short prose Upaniṣad of nineteen mantras transmitted in the Śukla Yajurveda tradition. In the Muktikā canon of 108 Upaniṣads it is number 53; among the twenty Yoga Upaniṣads it is the principal text on tāraka-yoga — the yoga of “that which carries one across.” Its central subject is the direct contemplation of inner light (jyoti-darśana) as the means to liberation.","Where the Brahma-sūtras, the kārikās of Gauḍapāda, and the major Upaniṣads provide the conceptual architecture of advaita, the Advaya-tāraka provides the operative method by which the non-dual is realised: a specific set of contemplations on inner and outer light, supported by the mudrās śāmbhavī and khecarī, and culminating in the state called amanaska (“no-mind”)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1.2  Title","title":"Three Senses of “Tāraka”","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The word tāraka (from √tṝ, “to cross over”) names the text’s central instrument. The Upaniṣad uses it in three nested senses, all of which the title carries.","<table><tr><td>Sense</td><td>Sanskrit reading</td><td>What it points to</td></tr><tr><td>1 · The agent</td><td>tārayati iti tārakaḥ</td><td>That which carries (one) across — i.e., the inner light which crosses the seeker beyond saṃsāra.</td></tr><tr><td>2 · The object</td><td>tārakaṃ jyotiḥ</td><td>The “Pole-star” light — a steady, self-luminous inner luminescence seen between or above the brows.</td></tr><tr><td>3 · The method</td><td>tāraka-yogaḥ</td><td>The discipline of contemplating that inner light. This is the text’s technical name for its own teaching.</td></tr></table>","Advaya (“non-dual”) qualifies tāraka: the deliverer is itself non-dual; the seer, the light, and the goal are not three. The title therefore reads: “the Upaniṣad of the non-dual deliverer.”"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1.3  Place in the Yoga Upaniṣads","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The twenty Yoga Upaniṣads form a stratum of late-classical / early-medieval texts (approximately 5th–12th c. CE, though precise dating is contested) that systematise yogic practice within the Upaniṣadic frame. Advaya-tāraka sits at the centre of this corpus on the side of jyoti-yoga (light-yoga) and amanaska-yoga (no-mind yoga).","<table><tr><td>Text</td><td>Principal focus</td><td>Relation to Advaya-tāraka</td></tr><tr><td>Yoga-tattva</td><td>Eight limbs of yoga</td><td>Provides the broader haṭha framework.</td></tr><tr><td>Dhyāna-bindu / Nāda-bindu / Amṛta-bindu</td><td>Bindu contemplation</td><td>Cognate light-contemplations.</td></tr><tr><td>Yoga-kuṇḍalinī</td><td>Kuṇḍalinī ascent</td><td>Treats the same suṣumnā Advaya-tāraka invokes in antar-lakṣya.</td></tr><tr><td>Tejo-bindu</td><td>Pure consciousness (tejas)</td><td>Closest in tone — both culminate in an advaitic recognition.</td></tr><tr><td>Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa</td><td>Five-fold ākāśa &amp; nine cakras</td><td>Cognate light-and-space meditation system.</td></tr><tr><td>Haṃsa, Brahma-vidyā, Kṣurikā, etc.</td><td>Various</td><td>Same corpus; share vocabulary.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1.4  Why This Text Repays Close Study","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["It is short. 19 mantras can be read attentively in an hour; the practice they prescribe is the work of a lifetime.","It is operational. Almost every mantra prescribes a specific contemplation, posture, gaze, or recognition. Unlike doctrinal Upaniṣads (Bṛhadāraṇyaka, Chāndogya), this text is a manual.","It contains the locus classicus of two famous yogic doctrines: the definition of guru (gu-ru = the dispeller of darkness, mantra 16) and the canonical statement of śāmbhavī-mudrā (mantra 14).","It bridges advaita and yoga without privileging either. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin reads it as advaitic; the haṭha-yoga lineage reads it as method. Both readings are present in the text itself."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2.1  The Two Tārakas","title":"Pūrva and Uttara","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Upaniṣad divides tāraka-yoga into two stages — an earlier (pūrva) stage with form, and a later (uttara) stage without form. Both are necessary; the second cannot be reached without traversing the first.","<table><tr><td>Stage</td><td>Sanskrit name</td><td>Object</td><td>State</td><td>Mudrā / aid</td><td>Reached when</td></tr><tr><td>Pūrva-tāraka</td><td>sa-mūrti (with form)</td><td>Inner / outer / middle lights (the three lakṣyas)</td><td>Concentration on luminous form</td><td>Śāmbhavī-mudrā</td><td>The light becomes steady and unbroken.</td></tr><tr><td>Uttara-tāraka</td><td>amanaska / a-mūrti (without form)</td><td>No object — pure awareness alone</td><td>Mano-laya (mind-dissolution); unmanī avasthā</td><td>No mudrā can compel it; arises spontaneously</td><td>Mind has been entirely surrendered into the light.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2.2  The Three Lakṣyas (Targets)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Pūrva-tāraka is operated through three “targets” of contemplation. Together these cover the entire field of luminous appearance.","<table><tr><td>Lakṣya</td><td>Locus</td><td>What is contemplated</td></tr><tr><td>Antar-lakṣya</td><td>Inside the body</td><td>The suṣumnā channel, the heart-jyoti, kuṇḍalinī, brahma-nāḍī, and the blue-flame (nīla-jyoti) at the brahma-randhra / between the brows.</td></tr><tr><td>Bahir-lakṣya</td><td>Outside the body, before the nose</td><td>Successive colour-spaces at 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 finger-widths in front — blue, yellow, red, white, ākāśa-like.</td></tr><tr><td>Madhya-lakṣya</td><td>Neither inside nor outside</td><td>The chid-ākāśa — the conscious space within which both inner and outer arise; the sūrya-koṭi-prabhā (light of ten million suns) seen as the substrate.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2.3  The Six Cakras Acknowledged (but not Central)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Upaniṣad recognises the cakra-system but does not centre its practice on cakra penetration. The cakras serve as anatomical landmarks for the lakṣyas — particularly the heart cakra (anāhata, locus of the inner jyoti) and the brow centre (ājñā, locus of the nīla-jyoti and śāmbhavī gaze).","<table><tr><td>Cakra</td><td>Location</td><td>Role in Advaya-tāraka practice</td></tr><tr><td>Mūlādhāra</td><td>Perineum</td><td>Seat of kuṇḍalinī, mentioned in antar-lakṣya.</td></tr><tr><td>Svādhiṣṭhāna</td><td>Genital region</td><td>Not centrally treated.</td></tr><tr><td>Maṇipūra</td><td>Navel</td><td>Subtle-fire locus, supports the rising jyoti.</td></tr><tr><td>Anāhata</td><td>Heart</td><td>Seat of the heart-jyoti; central in antar-lakṣya.</td></tr><tr><td>Viśuddha</td><td>Throat</td><td>Not centrally treated.</td></tr><tr><td>Ājñā</td><td>Between brows</td><td>Locus of nīla-jyoti; the śāmbhavī gaze focuses here.</td></tr><tr><td>(Sahasrāra / Brahma-randhra)</td><td>Crown</td><td>The exit-point; site of final jyoti-darśana.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2.4  Śāmbhavī and Khecarī","title":"the Two Operative Mudrās","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Śāmbhavī-mudrā (mantra 14) — antar-lakṣyam bahir-dṛṣṭir nimeṣa-unmeṣa-varjitā / eṣā sā śāmbhavī mudrā — “The (mind’s) target within; the gaze outward; free of blink. This is the śāmbhavī mudrā.” The mind rests on the inner light while the eyes remain open and unblinking. Outward appearance unchanged; inner attention wholly turned.</td></tr></table>","<table><tr><td>Khecarī-mudrā — In the Advaya-tāraka, khecarī is not the haṭha-yogic tongue-reversal but the “space-going” state of the mind: the mind itself moves in the chid-ākāśa, freed from determinate objects. The Upaniṣad uses khecarī as a synonym for the unmanī avasthā that opens onto uttara-tāraka.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2.5  Key Sanskrit Vocabulary","title":"","sa":[],"words":[["advaya","non-dual","Neither two — neither object nor subject independently."],["tāraka","that which crosses over","The inner light; also the practice on it."],["jyotis","light","Self-luminous awareness; the contemplative object of pūrva-tāraka."],["amanaska","no-mind","The end-state of uttara-tāraka. Not unconsciousness; the cessation of mental movement while awareness remains."],["unmanī","beyond-mind","Synonym of amanaska; the transition out of manas."],["lakṣya","target","The point of contemplative focus."],["cid-ākāśa","conscious space","The luminous interior in which all images appear."],["brahma-randhra","aperture of brahman","The crown opening; site of final jyoti-darśana."],["nīla-jyoti","blue light","The specific colouration of the ājñā-located light."],["kuṇḍalinī","the coiled one","The contained energy at mūlādhāra, source of the rising light."],["śāmbhavī","of/pertaining to Śambhu (Śiva)","The mudrā named for the seer who looks while remaining unmoved."],["khecarī","space-going","In this text, the mind’s freedom of motion within cid-ākāśa."]],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Part III · The Nineteen Mantras","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each mantra is given in Devanāgarī, IAST, with a literal word-by-word gloss, a translation, and a synthesis drawn from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā. Mantra numbering follows the standard edition (Adyar Library); some editions split or combine mantras, but the total content is unchanged."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1  ·  The Qualified Aspirant","title":"","sa":["अथ हैनं अद्वयतारकोपनिषदं व्याख्यास्यामः । जितेन्द्रियं जितमानसं जिताशनं जितनिद्रं जितशीतोष्णं जितमानावमानं षड्ऊर्मिवर्जितं षड्भाववर्जितं ब्रह्मचर्यगुरुसेवादिशास्त्रश्रवणपराङ्मुखत्वसम्पन्नं योगिनं समुपास्य गुरुं तस्मादद्वयतारकोपनिषदं विद्यां पृच्छेत् ॥१॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ advaya-tārakopaniṣadaṃ vyākhyāsyāmaḥ / jitendriyaṃ jita-mānasaṃ jita-aśanaṃ jita-nidraṃ jita-śīta-uṣṇaṃ jita-māna-avamānaṃ ṣaḍ-ūrmi-varjitaṃ ṣaḍ-bhāva-varjitaṃ brahma-carya-guru-sevā-ādi-śāstra-śravaṇa-parāṅmukhatva-saṃpannaṃ yoginaṃ samupāsya guruṃ tasmād advaya-tārakopaniṣadaṃ vidyāṃ pṛcchet //1//"],"words":[["atha hainaṃ","","now, then, this (Upaniṣad)"],["advaya-tārakopaniṣadaṃ","","the Advaya-Tāraka Upaniṣad"],["vyākhyāsyāmaḥ","","we shall expound"],["jitendriyaṃ","","one who has conquered the senses"],["jita-mānasaṃ","","conquered the mind"],["jita-aśanaṃ","","conquered (cravings for) food"],["jita-nidraṃ","","conquered sleep"],["jita-śīta-uṣṇaṃ","","conquered cold and heat"],["jita-māna-avamānaṃ","","conquered honour and dishonour"],["ṣaḍ-ūrmi-varjitaṃ","","free of the six waves (hunger, thirst, sorrow, delusion, old age, death)"],["ṣaḍ-bhāva-varjitaṃ","","free of the six modifications (birth, being, growth, change, decay, destruction)"],["brahma-carya","","celibacy"],["guru-sevā","","service of the teacher"],["ādi-śāstra-śravaṇa","","hearing of authoritative scripture"],["parāṅmukhatva-saṃpannaṃ","","endowed with turning-away (from worldly objects)"],["yoginaṃ samupāsya guruṃ","","having approached such a yogi-teacher"],["tasmāt","","from him"],["advaya-tārakopaniṣadaṃ vidyāṃ pṛcchet","","one should ask for the Advaya-Tāraka knowledge"]],"lit":"Now we shall expound the Advaya-Tāraka Upaniṣad. Approaching a teacher who is a yogin — one who has conquered the senses, the mind, food, sleep, heat-and-cold, and honour-and-dishonour; who is free of the six waves and the six modifications; who is endowed with celibacy, service of the teacher, study of scripture, and turning-away (from worldly objects) — from him one should ask for the Advaya-Tāraka knowledge.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The opening mantra is an adhikāra-paribhāṣā — a statement of qualification. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin notes: the teaching is not withheld, but the unprepared cannot receive it. The six waves (ūrmi) are the disturbances of the body — hunger, thirst, sorrow, delusion (of body-mind), old age, death — and the six modifications (bhāva-vikāra) are the transformations of existence — birth, being, growth, change, decay, destruction. The aspirant must be beyond the first six in vital terms (jita-aśana, jita-nidra) and disengaged from the second six in self-identification (ṣaḍ-bhāva-varjita)."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 2  ·  Definition of the Yogin","title":"","sa":["सर्वसङ्कल्पविकल्पवर्जितः सर्वद्वन्द्वविनिर्मुक्तः परब्रह्मनिश्चितमतिरेव योगी ॥२॥"],"iast":["sarva-saṃkalpa-vikalpa-varjitaḥ sarva-dvandva-vinirmuktaḥ para-brahma-niścita-matir eva yogī //2//"],"words":[["sarva-saṃkalpa-vikalpa-varjitaḥ","","free of all volition and doubt"],["sarva-dvandva-vinirmuktaḥ","","released from all opposites (heat-cold, honour-dishonour, pleasure-pain)"],["para-brahma-niścita-matiḥ","","whose mind is firmly settled on the supreme brahman"],["eva","","indeed"],["yogī","","is the yogin"]],"lit":"He alone is the yogin who is free of all volition and doubt, released from all dualities, and whose mind is firmly settled on the supreme brahman.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> A negative-and-positive definition. Yoga is not posture or breath-discipline here — those are presupposed in mantra 1 — but a settled mind. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin glosses niścita-matiḥ as the very nirvikalpa-samādhi later texts will name. Without this settled mind, the contemplations to follow (on the three lakṣyas) become merely visual exercises."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3  ·  Tāraka and Amanaska Distinguished","title":"","sa":["तारकं द्विविधम् — पूर्वं तारकमुत्तरं तारकं चेति । मूर्तिमत्तारकं पूर्वममूर्तिमत्तारकमुत्तरं तारकम् ॥३॥"],"iast":["tārakaṃ dvividham — pūrvaṃ tārakam uttaraṃ tārakaṃ ceti / mūrtimat-tārakaṃ pūrvam a-mūrtimat-tārakam uttaraṃ tārakam //3//"],"words":[["tārakaṃ dvividham","","tāraka is twofold"],["pūrvam tārakam","","the earlier tāraka"],["uttaraṃ tārakam","","the later tāraka"],["ca iti","","and (so it is)"],["mūrtimat-tārakaṃ","","tāraka-with-form"],["pūrvam","","is the earlier"],["a-mūrtimat-tārakam","","tāraka-without-form"],["uttaraṃ tārakam","","is the later tāraka"]],"lit":"Tāraka is of two kinds — the earlier and the later. The tāraka-with-form is the earlier; the tāraka-without-form is the later.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The crucial typology of the text. Pūrva-tāraka contemplates a determinate object — a luminous form, the inner light, the bindu. Uttara-tāraka is objectless: pure awareness without contemplative content. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin compares them to a boat (pūrva, used to cross the river of saṃsāra) and the further shore itself (uttara). The earlier is the means; the later is the end. One cannot reach the further shore without the boat, but at the further shore the boat is laid down."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ B  ·  Pūrva-Tāraka: The Three Lakṣyas  (Mantras 4–11)","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 4  ·  The Three Lakṣyas of Pūrva-Tāraka","title":"","sa":["यदिन्द्रियं मनोबुद्धिचित्ताहंकाराख्यान्तःकरणं तदात्मकमेव लक्ष्यत्रयं भवति — अन्तर्लक्ष्यं बहिर्लक्ष्यं मध्यलक्ष्यं चेति ॥४॥"],"iast":["yad indriyaṃ mano-buddhi-citta-ahaṃkārākhya-antaḥkaraṇaṃ tad-ātmakam eva lakṣya-trayaṃ bhavati — antar-lakṣyaṃ bahir-lakṣyaṃ madhya-lakṣyaṃ ca iti //4//"],"words":[["yat indriyaṃ","","that which is the sense-faculty"],["manas-buddhi-citta-ahaṃkāra-ākhya","","named manas, buddhi, citta, and ahaṃkāra"],["antaḥ-karaṇaṃ","","the inner instrument"],["tat-ātmakam eva","","its very nature (is the basis of)"],["lakṣya-trayaṃ bhavati","","— there are three lakṣyas (targets)"],["antar-lakṣyaṃ","","the internal target"],["bahir-lakṣyaṃ","","the external target"],["madhya-lakṣyaṃ ca iti","","and the middle target"]],"lit":"The very inner instrument — which comprises manas, buddhi, citta, and ahaṃkāra — provides the basis for three targets of contemplation: the internal, the external, and the middle.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The Upaniṣad places the three lakṣyas not in three regions of physical space but in three modes of the antaḥkaraṇa. Antar-lakṣya is the target as turned inward (introspective mode); bahir-lakṣya is the target as projected outward (sense-mode held open); madhya-lakṣya is the target as recognised in the chid-ākāśa that is neither inside nor outside (witness-mode). Each is a different stance of the same inner instrument toward the same light."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5  ·  Antar-Lakṣya","title":"Suṣumnā, Heart-Jyoti, Kuṇḍalinī","sa":["अन्तर्लक्ष्यं तु सुषुम्ना ब्रह्मनाडी अन्तर्ज्योतिर्मयी कुण्डलिनी ब्रह्मरूपिणी अन्तर्ज्योतिर्ब्रह्मरूपिणी इति ॥५॥"],"iast":["antar-lakṣyaṃ tu suṣumnā brahma-nāḍī antar-jyotir-mayī kuṇḍalinī brahma-rūpiṇī antar-jyotir-brahma-rūpiṇī iti //5//"],"words":[["antar-lakṣyaṃ tu","","as for the internal target"],["suṣumnā","","— the suṣumnā channel"],["brahma-nāḍī","","the brahman-channel (alternate name)"],["antar-jyotir-mayī","","made of inner light"],["kuṇḍalinī","","the coiled one"],["brahma-rūpiṇī","","of the form of brahman"],["antar-jyotir-brahma-rūpiṇī iti","","— having the form of inner-light-brahman, thus (it is)"]],"lit":"The internal target is the suṣumnā — the brahma-channel, made of inner light; the kuṇḍalinī, whose form is brahman; the inner light whose form is brahman.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The first articulation of antar-lakṣya. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin identifies four overlapping names for one reality: suṣumnā (the central channel), brahma-nāḍī (the same channel as brahman’s pathway), kuṇḍalinī (the coiled energy within the channel), and brahma-rūpiṇī (its identity with brahman). The four are not separate objects but four aspects of the single antar-jyotis. The contemplation is to see this light flowing or standing within the central axis."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6  ·  Antar-Lakṣya","title":"Brahma-Randhra and the Lights of Five Elements","sa":["भ्रूमध्ये नीलज्योतिर्ब्रह्मरूपं तत्र मनो लीयते सा अन्तर्लक्ष्यलक्षणावस्था । शिखामध्ये अमृतकुण्डलिनीशक्तिः कोटिसूर्यप्रकाशा कोटिचन्द्रप्रभा सा अन्तर्लक्ष्यलक्षणा ॥६॥"],"iast":["bhrū-madhye nīla-jyotir brahma-rūpaṃ tatra mano līyate sā antar-lakṣya-lakṣaṇa-avasthā / śikhā-madhye amṛta-kuṇḍalinī-śaktiḥ koṭi-sūrya-prakāśā koṭi-candra-prabhā sā antar-lakṣya-lakṣaṇā //6//"],"words":[["bhrū-madhye","","between the brows"],["nīla-jyotiḥ","","the blue light"],["brahma-rūpaṃ","","of the form of brahman"],["tatra mano līyate","","there the mind dissolves"],["sā","","that is"],["antar-lakṣya-lakṣaṇa-avasthā","","the state characterised as the internal target"],["śikhā-madhye","","in the middle of the crown-tuft (i.e., brahma-randhra)"],["amṛta-kuṇḍalinī-śaktiḥ","","the immortal kuṇḍalinī-power"],["koṭi-sūrya-prakāśā","","radiance of ten million suns"],["koṭi-candra-prabhā","","lustre of ten million moons"],["sā antar-lakṣya-lakṣaṇā","","— that is the characteristic of the internal target"]],"lit":"Between the brows is the blue light whose form is brahman; there the mind dissolves — that is the state characterised as the internal target. In the middle of the crown (brahma-randhra) is the immortal kuṇḍalinī-power, having the radiance of ten million suns and the lustre of ten million moons — that, too, is the mark of the internal target.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> Two further loci within antar-lakṣya are now specified — the ājñā (bhrū-madhya) and the sahasrāra (śikhā-madhya / brahma-randhra). At the ājñā the contemplative sees the steady blue flame; at the brahma-randhra, the overwhelming twin radiance. The phrase “there the mind dissolves” (tatra mano līyate) is operationally precise: it names mano-laya as the criterion that the contemplation has succeeded. Without mano-laya, what is seen is mere imagery."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 7  ·  Bahir-Lakṣya","title":"Lights in Front of the Eyes","sa":["नासाग्रे चतुर्भिः षड्भिरष्टभिः दशभिः द्वादशभिः क्रमशो अङ्गुलैः नीलश्यामरक्तपीतशुक्लाकाशवर्णैर्बहिर्लक्ष्यदर्शनम् । तदेव विहायसि भ्रमिते चलदृष्ट्या यो दृश्यते स पुरुषो ब्रह्मस्वरूपः ॥७॥"],"iast":["nāsāgre caturbhiḥ ṣaḍbhir aṣṭabhir daśabhir dvādaśabhiḥ kramaśo aṅgulaiḥ nīla-śyāma-rakta-pīta-śukla-ākāśa-varṇair bahir-lakṣya-darśanam / tad eva vihāyasi bhramite cala-dṛṣṭyā yo dṛśyate sa puruṣo brahma-svarūpaḥ //7//"],"words":[["nāsāgre","","at the tip of the nose / in front of the nose"],["caturbhiḥ ṣaḍbhiḥ aṣṭabhiḥ daśabhiḥ dvādaśabhiḥ","","by four, six, eight, ten, twelve"],["kramaśaḥ aṅgulaiḥ","","progressively in finger-widths"],["nīla-śyāma-rakta-pīta-śukla-ākāśa-varṇaiḥ","","of the colours blue, dark, red, yellow, white, and sky-coloured"],["bahir-lakṣya-darśanam","","— the seeing of the external target"],["tat eva vihāyasi","","and that very (light) in the sky"],["bhramite cala-dṛṣṭyā","","when the mobile gaze wanders (over it)"],["yo dṛśyate","","that which is seen"],["sa puruṣaḥ","","is the puruṣa"],["brahma-svarūpaḥ","","of the very form of brahman"]],"lit":"In front of the nose, at four, six, eight, ten, and twelve finger-widths progressively, are seen colours — blue, dark, red, yellow, white, and sky-coloured — this is the seeing of the external target. And that same (light) seen in the sky as the gaze moves — that seen one is the puruṣa, of the very form of brahman.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The locus classicus of bahir-lakṣya. Note the technique: the gaze is fixed before the nose, at varying distances, and the practitioner observes spontaneously arising colour-spaces. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin emphasises that these are not imagined: they arise to the steady gaze of the meditator who has stabilised antar-lakṣya. The puruṣa seen in the sky-space when the gaze is mobile is identical with the inner light; the difference is only the stance of the antaḥkaraṇa. Some editions number 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 anguli as five levels; others vary; the principle is the same."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 8  ·  Bahir-Lakṣya","title":"Looking Above the Head","sa":["ऊर्ध्वमुत्थाने ऊर्ध्वदृष्ट्या चलज्योतिर्दृष्टमप्यनुदृष्टं भवति । तदेव वैष्णवं पदम् ॥८॥"],"iast":["ūrdhvam utthāne ūrdhva-dṛṣṭyā cala-jyotir-dṛṣṭam api anudṛṣṭaṃ bhavati / tad eva vaiṣṇavaṃ padam //8//"],"words":[["ūrdhvam utthāne","","rising upward"],["ūrdhva-dṛṣṭyā","","with upward gaze"],["cala-jyotiḥ","","the moving light"],["dṛṣṭam api","","though (only briefly) seen"],["anudṛṣṭaṃ bhavati","","becomes ever-seen / continuously perceived"],["tat eva","","that alone"],["vaiṣṇavaṃ padam","","is the supreme abode of Viṣṇu (the pervading)"]],"lit":"When (the practitioner) rises and gazes upward, the moving light — though only briefly seen at first — becomes continuously perceived. That alone is the Vaiṣṇava-pada (the supreme abode).","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> A second bahir-lakṣya technique: standing or seated with the head erect, the gaze drawn upward. The Upaniṣad uses the term vaiṣṇavaṃ padam — Viṣṇu’s supreme step — citing the Ṛg-vedic image (RV 1.22.20: tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam) and identifying it with the continuously-perceived inner light. The discipline of vision shifts the light from intermittent flash (dṛṣṭam) to steady presence (anudṛṣṭam). This anudṛṣṭa-jyotis is the mark that pūrva-tāraka is maturing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 9  ·  Madhya-Lakṣya","title":"The Five Ākāśas Within","sa":["मध्यलक्ष्यं तु प्रातःकालादिमारब्ध्य पञ्चविधं चित्राकाशं चिदाकाशं परमाकाशं तत्त्वाकाशं सूर्याकाशं चेति । तेषु प्रत्येकं प्रकाशो भासते स मध्यलक्ष्यलक्षणं ॥९॥"],"iast":["madhya-lakṣyaṃ tu prātaḥ-kālādim ārabhya pañca-vidhaṃ citra-ākāśaṃ cid-ākāśaṃ parama-ākāśaṃ tattva-ākāśaṃ sūrya-ākāśaṃ ca iti / teṣu pratyekaṃ prakāśo bhāsate sa madhya-lakṣya-lakṣaṇaṃ //9//"],"words":[["madhya-lakṣyaṃ tu","","the middle target, then"],["prātaḥ-kāla-ādim ārabhya","","beginning from dawn-time onward"],["pañca-vidhaṃ","","of five kinds"],["citra-ākāśaṃ","","variegated space"],["cid-ākāśaṃ","","conscious space"],["parama-ākāśaṃ","","supreme space"],["tattva-ākāśaṃ","","space of the (twenty-five) principles"],["sūrya-ākāśaṃ ca iti","","and the sun-space"],["teṣu pratyekaṃ","","in each of these"],["prakāśaḥ bhāsate","","a luminosity shines"],["sa madhya-lakṣya-lakṣaṇaṃ","","— that is the mark of the middle target"]],"lit":"The middle target, beginning from dawn-time onward, is fivefold: citra-ākāśa (variegated), cid-ākāśa (conscious), parama-ākāśa (supreme), tattva-ākāśa (space of principles), and sūrya-ākāśa (sun-space). In each, a distinct luminosity shines — that is the mark of the middle target.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The five ākāśas are the famous classification of inner space. Citra-ākāśa is the field of mental imagery; cid-ākāśa is bare conscious space; parama-ākāśa is the unconditioned space identified with brahman; tattva-ākāśa is the space of the tattvas (the ontology of Sāṃkhya/Yoga); sūrya-ākāśa is the dazzling space identified with the inner sun. The practitioner, especially at dawn (prātaḥ-kāla), is invited to discriminate these five. The Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad develops the same scheme in greater detail."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 10  ·  Madhya-Lakṣya","title":"The Light Like Ten Million Suns","sa":["चिदाकाशे कोटिसूर्यप्रकाशं दृश्यते स एव परमात्मा । तस्मिन् ध्याने योगी सिद्ध्यति ॥१०॥"],"iast":["cid-ākāśe koṭi-sūrya-prakāśaṃ dṛśyate sa eva paramātmā / tasmin dhyāne yogī sidhyati //10//"],"words":[["cid-ākāśe","","in the conscious space"],["koṭi-sūrya-prakāśaṃ","","a radiance of ten million suns"],["dṛśyate","","is seen"],["saḥ eva paramātmā","","that itself is the supreme self"],["tasmin dhyāne","","in that contemplation"],["yogī sidhyati","","the yogi is perfected"]],"lit":"In the cid-ākāśa is seen a radiance of ten million suns — that is the supreme Self. By that contemplation the yogi is perfected.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The peak of pūrva-tāraka. The radiance is not symbolic — it is described as actually seen within the conscious space. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin glosses: the “sun” is not the physical sun but the self-luminous awareness itself, recognised when the discriminations of the previous mantras have settled. Tasmin dhyāne yogī sidhyati names this as the perfecting (siddhi) of the yoga."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 11  ·  Sequence","title":"Antar, Bahir, Madhya Together","sa":["एवं त्रिविधलक्ष्यदर्शनाभ्यासेन ब्रह्मणि सायुज्यं ब्रह्मसाक्षात्कारो जायते । तं विदित्वैव योगी कृतकृत्यः ॥११॥"],"iast":["evaṃ tri-vidha-lakṣya-darśana-abhyāsena brahmaṇi sāyujyaṃ brahma-sākṣātkāro jāyate / taṃ viditvaiva yogī kṛta-kṛtyaḥ //11//"],"words":[["evaṃ","","thus"],["tri-vidha-lakṣya-darśana-abhyāsena","","by practice on the three-fold targets"],["brahmaṇi sāyujyaṃ","","union (sāyujya) with brahman"],["brahma-sākṣātkāraḥ jāyate","","the direct experience of brahman arises"],["taṃ viditvā eva","","having known that alone"],["yogī kṛta-kṛtyaḥ","","the yogi has accomplished what is to be done"]],"lit":"Thus by the practice of contemplating the three-fold targets, union (sāyujya) with brahman — the direct experience of brahman — arises. Having known that alone, the yogi has accomplished all that is to be done.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> Closure of the pūrva-tāraka section. The three lakṣyas, taken together, yield brahma-sākṣātkāra — direct experience, not inferential conviction. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin notes the verb sāyujyaṃ rather than sārūpya or sālokya: not the heaven-going union, but the deepest mode — identity. The phrase kṛta-kṛtyaḥ (“one for whom all that is to be done is done”) signals the boundary between pūrva and uttara tāraka."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ C  ·  Uttara-Tāraka, Śāmbhavī, Khecarī  (Mantras 12–15)","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 12  ·  Uttara-Tāraka","title":"Amanaska, the Formless","sa":["अमूर्तितारकमुत्तरं तदुत्तरं तारकमप्यमनस्कमित्युच्यते । उन्मनी अवस्था यत्र चित्तमुपरमते सा परमपदप्राप्तिः ॥१२॥"],"iast":["a-mūrti-tārakam uttaraṃ tad uttaraṃ tārakam api amanaskam ity ucyate / unmanī avasthā yatra cittam uparamate sā parama-pada-prāptiḥ //12//"],"words":[["a-mūrti-tārakam uttaraṃ","","tāraka-without-form is the later (tāraka)"],["tat uttaraṃ tārakam","","that later tāraka"],["api amanaskam iti ucyate","","is also called the “no-mind”"],["unmanī avasthā","","the beyond-mind state"],["yatra cittam uparamate","","wherein the citta comes to rest"],["sā parama-pada-prāptiḥ","","that is the attainment of the supreme abode"]],"lit":"The later tāraka is formless; that later tāraka is also called amanaska (no-mind). The unmanī state, wherein the citta comes to rest, is the attainment of the supreme abode.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The transition. Pūrva-tāraka has been operated through luminous forms; uttara-tāraka has no form — no lakṣya at all. The Upaniṣad introduces two technical terms: amanaska (the condition without mind-activity) and unmanī (the state above mind). They are not identical in later technical usage — unmanī is sometimes the gateway, amanaska the final state — but in this text they are interchangeable. The criterion is cittam uparamate: the activity of citta has ceased."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 13  ·  Uttara-Tāraka Continued","title":"Eyes Half-Closed, Mind Steady","sa":["अर्धोन्मीलितनेत्रः स्थिरमानसो नासाग्रन्यस्तदृष्टिः चन्द्रसूर्यापि अवलोकनात् निवृत्तस्तेजसः परं तत्त्वमुद्दीप्यमानम् दृष्ट्वा तत्स्वरूपेण भवति । तदद्वैतं तदाख्यं तारकम् ॥१३॥"],"iast":["ardha-unmīlita-netraḥ sthira-mānaso nāsāgra-nyasta-dṛṣṭiḥ candra-sūryāv api avalokanāt nivṛttas tejasaḥ paraṃ tattvam uddīpyamānam dṛṣṭvā tat-svarūpeṇa bhavati / tad advaitaṃ tad-ākhyaṃ tārakam //13//"],"words":[["ardha-unmīlita-netraḥ","","with eyes half-opened"],["sthira-mānasaḥ","","of steady mind"],["nāsāgra-nyasta-dṛṣṭiḥ","","gaze placed on the tip of the nose"],["candra-sūryau api","","even moon and sun"],["avalokanāt nivṛttaḥ","","having withdrawn from seeing"],["tejasaḥ param tattvam","","the supreme principle beyond (all) lights"],["uddīpyamānam","","blazing"],["dṛṣṭvā","","having seen"],["tat-svarūpeṇa bhavati","","one becomes of that very nature"],["tad advaitaṃ","","that is the non-dual"],["tad-ākhyaṃ tārakam","","that is named the tāraka"]],"lit":"With eyes half-opened, mind steady, gaze fixed on the nose-tip, withdrawn even from seeing sun and moon — having seen the supreme principle blazing beyond all lights, one becomes of that very nature. That is the non-dual; that is named the tāraka.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The bridge mantra between pūrva and uttara. The posture (half-opened eyes, gaze on nose) is operationally pūrva-tāraka; but the result (withdrawn from candra-sūrya, identification with the para-tattvam beyond all light) is uttara. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin reads “beyond all lights” (tejasaḥ paraṃ) as the decisive criterion: the practitioner has transcended even the inner sun (sūrya-koṭi-prabhā) of mantra 10. What is now recognised is not a light among lights, but the awareness in which lights appear."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 14  ·  Śāmbhavī-Mudrā","title":"The Locus Classicus","sa":["अन्तर्लक्ष्यं बहिर्दृष्टिर्निमेषोन्मेषवर्जिता । एषा सा शाम्भवी मुद्रा वेदशास्त्रेषु गोपिता ॥१४॥"],"iast":["antar-lakṣyaṃ bahir-dṛṣṭir nimeṣa-unmeṣa-varjitā / eṣā sā śāmbhavī mudrā vedaśāstreṣu gopitā //14//"],"words":[["antar-lakṣyaṃ","","the (mind’s) target is within"],["bahir-dṛṣṭiḥ","","the gaze is outward"],["nimeṣa-unmeṣa-varjitā","","free of blink (closing and opening of eyes)"],["eṣā sā śāmbhavī mudrā","","this is that śāmbhavī mudrā"],["vedaśāstreṣu gopitā","","— hidden in the Vedas and the scriptures"]],"lit":"The (mind’s) target within; the gaze without; free of blinking — this is that śāmbhavī mudrā, kept hidden in the Vedas and the scriptures.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The most-quoted mantra of the Upaniṣad. Śāmbhavī-mudrā is the simultaneous holding of inner attention and outer-fixed unblinking gaze. Quoted in Haṭha-yoga-pradīpikā 4.36, Gheraṇḍa-saṃhitā 3.64, and almost every later yoga manual. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin comments: the eyes appear open to the world, but the mind is not where they look. This is the bodily form of the same advaita — outer and inner held without contradiction. Gopitā (“hidden”) is not concealment but the inner-only accessibility: this mudrā cannot be performed by the body alone; the mind must already be in antar-lakṣya."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 15  ·  Khecarī-Mudrā and the Yogi’s Bliss","title":"","sa":["खेचरीमुद्रावस्थितः परं ब्रह्म साक्षात्करोति आत्मतेजसा । तस्य ज्ञानं सिद्धम् ॥१५॥"],"iast":["khecarī-mudrā-avasthitaḥ paraṃ brahma sākṣāt-karoti ātma-tejasā / tasya jñānaṃ siddham //15//"],"words":[["khecarī-mudrā-avasthitaḥ","","established in khecarī mudrā"],["paraṃ brahma","","the supreme brahman"],["sākṣāt-karoti","","directly realises"],["ātma-tejasā","","by the radiance of the Self"],["tasya jñānaṃ siddham","","his knowledge is accomplished"]],"lit":"Established in khecarī-mudrā, by the radiance of the Self, one directly realises the supreme brahman. His knowledge is accomplished.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> Khecarī here is the “space-going” state of the mind, not the haṭha-yogic tongue-reversal (though that practice is named the same in Haṭha-yoga-pradīpikā 3). The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin distinguishes the two: in this Upaniṣad khecarī is the mind moving freely in cid-ākāśa, having dropped its object-bound habits. The ātma-tejas — the Self’s own radiance — is no longer an object of contemplation but the medium of all recognition."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ D  ·  The Guru  (Mantras 16–18)","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 16  ·  The Definition of Guru","title":"gu and ru","sa":["गुशब्दस्त्वन्धकारः स्यात् रुशब्दस्तन्निरोधकः । अन्धकारनिरोधित्वात् गुरुरित्यभिधीयते ॥१६॥"],"iast":["gu-śabdas tv andhakāraḥ syāt ru-śabdas tan-nirodhakaḥ / andhakāra-nirodhitvāt gurur ity abhidhīyate //16//"],"words":[["gu-śabdaḥ tu","","the syllable “gu,” indeed"],["andhakāraḥ syāt","","should be (understood as) darkness"],["ru-śabdaḥ","","the syllable “ru”"],["tat-nirodhakaḥ","","is its remover"],["andhakāra-nirodhitvāt","","because of being the remover of darkness"],["guruḥ iti abhidhīyate","","— (one) is called “guru”"]],"lit":"The syllable gu is darkness; the syllable ru is its remover. Because of (being the) remover of darkness, one is called guru.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The locus classicus of this universally-known definition. It is here in the Advaya-tāraka — not in the Bhagavad-gītā, not in the Yoga-sūtra, not in the Upaniṣads usually cited — that this nirukti (etymological reading) is first preserved. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin notes its placement: only after the practitioner has been through the lakṣyas, śāmbhavī, and khecarī is the full meaning of guru disclosed. The guru is not primarily the human teacher but the principle of darkness-dispelling — which the human teacher embodies and the inner light is."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 17  ·  Guru as Brahman, the Highest Refuge","title":"","sa":["गुरुरेव परं ब्रह्म गुरुरेव परा गतिः । गुरुरेव परा विद्या गुरुरेव परायणम् ॥१७॥"],"iast":["gurur eva paraṃ brahma gurur eva parā gatiḥ / gurur eva parā vidyā gurur eva parāyaṇam //17//"],"words":[["guruḥ eva","","the guru alone"],["paraṃ brahma","","is the supreme brahman"],["guruḥ eva parā gatiḥ","","the guru alone is the supreme refuge"],["guruḥ eva parā vidyā","","the guru alone is the supreme knowledge"],["guruḥ eva parāyaṇam","","the guru alone is the supreme abode/goal"]],"lit":"The guru alone is the supreme brahman; the guru alone is the supreme refuge; the guru alone is the supreme knowledge; the guru alone is the supreme abode.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The four-fold predication identifies guru with brahman as paraṃ, gatiḥ, vidyā, and parāyaṇam — the supreme being, the supreme refuge, the supreme knowledge, and the supreme goal. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin: this is not flattery of a person. Guru, brahman, and the inner light are one. The mantra is to be contemplated, not merely recited."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 18  ·  Guru as the Witness and the Sole Reality","title":"","sa":["गुरुरेव परं तत्त्वं गुरुरेव परं तपः । गुरोः परतरं नास्ति त्रिषु लोकेषु विद्यते ॥१८॥"],"iast":["gurur eva paraṃ tattvaṃ gurur eva paraṃ tapaḥ / guroḥ parataraṃ nāsti triṣu lokeṣu vidyate //18//"],"words":[["guruḥ eva","","the guru alone"],["paraṃ tattvaṃ","","is the supreme principle"],["guruḥ eva paraṃ tapaḥ","","the guru alone is the supreme ascesis"],["guroḥ parataraṃ","","than/beyond the guru"],["na asti","","there is not"],["triṣu lokeṣu vidyate","","— (anything) found in the three worlds"]],"lit":"The guru alone is the supreme principle; the guru alone is the supreme ascesis. There is nothing higher than the guru anywhere in the three worlds.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> Closure of the guru-section. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin reads this not as triumphalist overstatement but as the natural consequence of mantra 16: if the guru is precisely andhakāra-nirodhitva — the function of darkness-removal itself — then nothing can be higher than the guru because nothing else operates that function. To seek beyond the guru is to seek beyond the very removal of ignorance, which is incoherent."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ E  ·  Phala-śruti  (Mantra 19)","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 19  ·  Concluding Mantra","title":"He Who Knows This","sa":["य इमामद्वयतारकोपनिषदं नित्यमधीते सोऽग्निपूतो भवति । स वायुपूतो भवति । स आदित्यपूतो भवति । स ब्रह्मपूतो भवति । स विष्णुपूतो भवति । स रुद्रपूतो भवति । स सर्ववेदपूतो भवति । स सर्वेषु तीर्थेषु स्नातो भवति । तेन सर्वे यज्ञा अनुष्ठिता भवन्ति । अष्टसहस्रा गायत्र्यो जपिता भवन्ति । तेन गायत्र्या लक्षजपफलमवाप्नोति । स सहस्ररुद्रजपफलमवाप्नोति । स पञ्चमहापातकेभ्यः पूतो भवति । स ब्रह्मलोके महीयते । इत्युपनिषत् ॥१९॥"],"iast":["ya imām advaya-tārakopaniṣadaṃ nityam adhīte so’gni-pūto bhavati / sa vāyu-pūto bhavati / sa āditya-pūto bhavati / sa brahma-pūto bhavati / sa viṣṇu-pūto bhavati / sa rudra-pūto bhavati / sa sarva-veda-pūto bhavati / sa sarveṣu tīrtheṣu snāto bhavati / tena sarve yajñā anuṣṭhitā bhavanti / aṣṭa-sahasrā gāyatryo japitā bhavanti / tena gāyatryā lakṣa-japa-phalam avāpnoti / sa sahasra-rudra-japa-phalam avāpnoti / sa pañca-mahā-pātakebhyaḥ pūto bhavati / sa brahma-loke mahīyate / ity-upaniṣat //19//"],"words":[["yaḥ imām","","whoever (recites/studies) this"],["advaya-tārakopaniṣadaṃ","","the Advaya-Tāraka Upaniṣad"],["nityam adhīte","","daily studies"],["saḥ agni-pūtaḥ bhavati","","he becomes purified by fire"],["vāyu-pūtaḥ","","purified by wind"],["āditya-pūtaḥ","","purified by the sun"],["brahma-pūtaḥ","","purified by Brahmā"],["viṣṇu-pūtaḥ","","purified by Viṣṇu"],["rudra-pūtaḥ","","purified by Rudra"],["sarva-veda-pūtaḥ","","purified by all the Vedas"],["sarveṣu tīrtheṣu snātaḥ","","bathed in all sacred fords"],["tena sarve yajñā anuṣṭhitāḥ","","by him all the sacrifices are performed"],["aṣṭa-sahasrāḥ gāyatryaḥ japitāḥ","","eight thousand Gāyatrīs are japa-d"],["gāyatryā lakṣa-japa-phalam","","the fruit of one hundred thousand Gāyatrī-japas"],["sahasra-rudra-japa-phalam","","the fruit of a thousand Rudra-japas"],["pañca-mahā-pātakebhyaḥ pūtaḥ","","purified of the five great sins"],["brahma-loke mahīyate","","is honoured in brahma-loka"],["iti upaniṣat","","— thus the Upaniṣad"]],"lit":"Whoever daily studies this Advaya-Tāraka Upaniṣad becomes purified by fire, wind, sun, Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Rudra; purified by all the Vedas; bathed in all sacred fords; (it is as if) all sacrifices have been performed by him; eight thousand Gāyatrīs japa-d; he obtains the fruit of a hundred thousand Gāyatrī-japas and a thousand Rudra-japas; he is purified of the five great sins; he is honoured in brahma-loka. Thus the Upaniṣad.","para":"","coms":[["Commentary","<em>(synth. from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin)</em> The phala-śruti (declaration of fruit). The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin reads this not as simple promise of reward but as a measured statement: the text is functionally equivalent to the great purificatory ritual acts. Reciting the Upaniṣad daily is itself the sacrificial action. The eight thousand Gāyatrīs is a specific ritual number; the lakṣa-japa-phala is the standard maximum-merit measure. The five great sins (pañca-mahā-pātaka) are: killing a brahmin, drinking intoxicants, theft, adultery with a guru’s wife, and associating with such persons. The final ity-upaniṣat closes the text."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Part IV · Visual Schemata","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Six diagrams summarising the architecture of the practice. ASCII-rendered in monospace so they remain stable across Word versions."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.1  The Two Tārakas (Path-Map)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │                       TĀRAKA-YOGA                              │\n   └─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┘\n                             │\n              ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐\n              ▼                              ▼\n   ┌────────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────────┐\n   │  PŪRVA-TĀRAKA      │  ───►   │  UTTARA-TĀRAKA       │\n   │  (with form)       │ ripens  │  (without form)      │\n   │                    │  into   │   = AMANASKA         │\n   │  Object: lights    │         │   = UNMANĪ avasthā   │\n   │  Method: 3 lakṣyas │         │   Object: none       │\n   │  Mudrā: śāmbhavī   │         │   Method: drop self  │\n   │                    │         │   Mudrā: khecarī     │\n   └────────┬───────────┘         └─────────┬───────────┘\n            │                                │\n   ┌────────┼───────────┐                    │\n   ▼        ▼           ▼                    ▼\n antar    bahir       madhya              brahma-sākṣātkāra\n lakṣya   lakṣya      lakṣya              (no longer a lakṣya;\n (inside) (in front)  (chid-ākāśa)         the seer is the seen)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.2  The Three Lakṣyas (Pūrva-Tāraka)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n                   ┌──────────────────────────┐\n                   │     PŪRVA-TĀRAKA         │\n                   │       Three Lakṣyas      │\n                   └──────────────┬───────────┘\n        ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐\n        ▼                         ▼                         ▼\n ┌────────────┐           ┌────────────┐           ┌────────────┐\n │  ANTAR     │           │  BAHIR     │           │  MADHYA    │\n │  (inside)  │           │  (in front)│           │ (chid-     │\n │            │           │            │           │  ākāśa)    │\n ├────────────┤           ├────────────┤           ├────────────┤\n │ suṣumnā    │           │ 4-aṅgula:  │           │ citra-     │\n │ brahma-    │           │   nīla     │           │  ākāśa     │\n │  nāḍī      │           │ 6-aṅgula:  │           │ cid-       │\n │ kuṇḍalinī  │           │   śyāma    │           │  ākāśa     │\n │ heart-     │           │ 8-aṅgula:  │           │ parama-    │\n │  jyoti     │           │   rakta    │           │  ākāśa     │\n │ ājñā-      │           │ 10-aṅgula: │           │ tattva-    │\n │  nīla-     │           │   pīta     │           │  ākāśa     │\n │  jyoti     │           │ 12-aṅgula: │           │ sūrya-     │\n │ brahma-    │           │   śukla    │           │  ākāśa     │\n │  randhra   │           │ + ākāśa-   │           │            │\n │            │           │   varṇa    │           │ Light:     │\n │            │           │            │           │ koṭi-sūrya │\n │            │           │ + ūrdhva-  │           │  prabhā    │\n │            │           │   dṛṣṭi    │           │            │\n │            │           │   = vaiṣ-  │           │            │\n │            │           │   ṇavaṃ    │           │            │\n │            │           │   padam    │           │            │\n └────────────┘           └────────────┘           └────────────┘\n   Mantras 5–6              Mantra  7–8              Mantra  9–10</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.3  Antar-Lakṣya","title":"The Inner Axis","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   ────────────────────────────────────────────  brahma-randhra\n                     ▲                            (śikhā-madhye)\n                     │                            koṭi-sūrya-prabhā\n                     │                            koṭi-candra-prabhā\n                     │                            (mantra 6, second half)\n                ┌────┴────┐\n                │  ājñā   │   ────────────────────  bhrū-madhya\n                │ (between│                            nīla-jyotis\n                │  brows) │                            &quot;mind dissolves here&quot;\n                └────┬────┘                            (mantra 6, first half)\n                     │\n                     │\n                ┌────┴────┐\n                │ viśuddha│\n                └────┬────┘\n                     │\n                ┌────┴────┐\n                │ anāhata │   ────────────────────  heart-jyoti\n                │ (heart) │                            antar-jyotis\n                └────┬────┘                            (mantra 5)\n                     │\n                ┌────┴────┐\n                │maṇipūra │\n                └────┬────┘\n                     │\n                ┌────┴────┐\n                │svādhi-  │\n                │ṣṭhāna   │\n                └────┬────┘\n                     │\n                ┌────┴────┐\n                │mūlādhāra│   ────────────────────  kuṇḍalinī\n                └─────────┘                            &quot;the coiled one&quot;\n                                                      brahma-rūpiṇī\n                                                      (mantra 5)\n\n   The single suṣumnā / brahma-nāḍī running through all six cakras —\n   contemplated as a continuous antar-jyotis.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.4  Bahir-Lakṣya","title":"Colour-Spaces in Front of the Nose","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n                          ↑ (also ūrdhva-dṛṣṭi: vaiṣṇavaṃ padam)\n                          │\n                          │\n            12 aṅgula  ▒▒▒▒▒  ākāśa-varṇa (sky-coloured)\n                          │\n            10 aṅgula  ░░░░░  śukla (white)\n                          │\n             8 aṅgula  ▓▓▓▓▓  rakta (red)\n                          │\n             6 aṅgula  ████  pīta (yellow)\n                          │\n             4 aṅgula  ▒▒▒▒  nīla (blue)\n                          │\n                       [ ◉ ] ← nāsāgra (tip of nose / point before)\n                       /   \\\n                      /     \\\n                     (open eyes, gaze fixed forward)\n\n   Reading-order varies slightly across editions; principle invariant:\n   light-spaces appear at progressive distances when the gaze stabilises.\n\n   Mantra 7 (colour-spaces)  ·  Mantra 8 (upward gaze → vaiṣṇavaṃ padam)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.5  Madhya-Lakṣya","title":"The Five Ākāśas","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   The interior conscious space is discriminated into five &quot;ākāśas&quot;.\n   These are stances of attention, not five different objects.\n\n   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │                                                              │\n   │   ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════╗      │\n   │   ║  5 · SŪRYA-ĀKĀŚA  — koṭi-sūrya-prabhā substrate    ║      │\n   │   ║                                                   ║      │\n   │   ║   ╔════════════════════════════════════════════╗  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║  4 · TATTVA-ĀKĀŚA  — space of 25 tattvas    ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║                                            ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ╔═════════════════════════════════════╗  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║  3 · PARAMA-ĀKĀŚA — unconditioned    ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║                                     ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ╔══════════════════════════════╗  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║  2 · CID-ĀKĀŚA — bare        ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║      conscious space         ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║                              ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║   ┌────────────────────────┐ ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║   │ 1 · CITRA-ĀKĀŚA        │ ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║   │     variegated         │ ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║   │     mental imagery      │ ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ║   └────────────────────────┘ ║  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ║   ╚══════════════════════════════╝  ║  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ║   ╚═════════════════════════════════════╝  ║  ║      │\n   │   ║   ╚════════════════════════════════════════════╝  ║      │\n   │   ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════╝      │\n   │                                                              │\n   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                                            Mantra 9–10\n\n   The deeper one penetrates, the more luminous and undifferentiated.\n   Sūrya-ākāśa is the substrate of all the rest.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.6  Śāmbhavī-Mudrā","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   The eyes:                     ●  open\n                                ◐  unblinking\n                                →  gaze fixed outward, far / nāsāgra\n   The mind:                    ←  attention turned inward to inner jyoti\n   The result:                  the same awareness holding both stances\n                                without conflict.\n\n      ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n      │                                                      │\n      │      ATTENTION (mind)            GAZE (eyes)         │\n      │      ←─────────────              ─────────────→      │\n      │                                                      │\n      │   antar-lakṣya              bahir-dṛṣṭi              │\n      │   (inner light)             (outward, unmoving)       │\n      │                                                      │\n      │                  nimeṣa-unmeṣa-varjita                │\n      │                  (free of blinking)                   │\n      │                                                      │\n      └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n   Mantra 14:  &quot;antar-lakṣyaṃ bahir-dṛṣṭir nimeṣa-unmeṣa-varjitā /\n                 eṣā sā śāmbhavī mudrā vedaśāstreṣu gopitā&quot;\n\n   The body shows nothing; the inner stance has fully changed.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.7  Path-Map","title":"Mantra to Mantra","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>\n   1   ──── adhikāra (qualification)\n   2   ──── yogin defined\n   3   ──── tāraka twofold: pūrva (with form) / uttara (formless)\n   4   ──── three lakṣyas introduced\n   5   ──── antar-lakṣya: suṣumnā, kuṇḍalinī, brahma-rūpiṇī\n   6   ──── antar-lakṣya: ājñā (nīla-jyotis), brahma-randhra (koṭi-sūrya)\n   7   ──── bahir-lakṣya: five colour-spaces in front of nose\n   8   ──── bahir-lakṣya: upward gaze, vaiṣṇavaṃ padam\n   9   ──── madhya-lakṣya: five ākāśas\n   10  ──── madhya-lakṣya: koṭi-sūrya-prabhā in cid-ākāśa\n   11  ──── three-lakṣya practice → brahma-sākṣātkāra\n   12  ──── transition: uttara-tāraka = amanaska = unmanī\n   13  ──── posture + result: identification with &quot;beyond all lights&quot;\n   14  ──── ŚĀMBHAVĪ-MUDRĀ  (locus classicus)\n   15  ──── KHECARĪ-MUDRĀ  (mind in cid-ākāśa)\n   16  ──── GURU: gu = darkness, ru = its remover\n   17  ──── guru = brahman, refuge, knowledge, abode\n   18  ──── nothing higher than guru in the three worlds\n   19  ──── phala-śruti (declaration of fruit)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Part V · Practical Steps & Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>A note on tone — The Upaniṣad is itself an operational manual. What follows is a reconstruction of its own sequence of steps, not invention. Italicised passages are observations drawn from the text or from Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā; non-italicised passages are practical derivations a reader can apply.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.1  Qualifications (Adhikāra)","title":"Before Practice","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Mantra 1 lists qualifications. They are not preconditions in the sense of “without them you cannot start” — they are conditions in the sense of “without them what is gained is unstable.” Take them as targets, not gatekeepers.","<table><tr><td>Qualification</td><td>Operational meaning</td></tr><tr><td>jita-indriya</td><td>The senses do not lead — you can turn from any object without struggle.</td></tr><tr><td>jita-mānasa</td><td>The mind does not run — you can hold attention on one thing for the length of a practice session.</td></tr><tr><td>jita-aśana</td><td>Eating regulated — neither indulgence nor mortification.</td></tr><tr><td>jita-nidra</td><td>Sleep regulated — you wake when intended, neither sleep-deprived nor over-slept.</td></tr><tr><td>jita-śīta-uṣṇa</td><td>Climatic comfort does not determine where you sit.</td></tr><tr><td>jita-māna-avamāna</td><td>Praise and blame both leave the mind unmoved.</td></tr><tr><td>ṣaḍ-ūrmi-varjita</td><td>Hunger, thirst, sorrow, delusion, aging, death — these are recognised as bodily phenomena, not \"mine\".</td></tr><tr><td>brahma-carya</td><td>Conservation of vital force; reduction of distractive desires.</td></tr><tr><td>guru-sevā</td><td>Continuing relationship with a teacher.</td></tr><tr><td>śāstra-śravaṇa</td><td>Sustained study of scripture.</td></tr><tr><td>parāṅmukhatva</td><td>Inner turning-away from worldly preoccupation; not necessarily outward renunciation.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.2  The Sequence of Practice","title":"As the Upaniṣad Lays it Out","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Sit in a stable posture. (The text does not prescribe a particular āsana; any steady seated position will do.)","Approach the three lakṣyas in order: antar first, bahir second, madhya third.","Antar-lakṣya. Eyes closed or half-closed. Contemplate (a) the suṣumnā channel running through the central axis from mūlādhāra to brahma-randhra; (b) the heart-jyoti at anāhata; (c) the nīla-jyoti between the brows; (d) the brilliant twin radiance at the brahma-randhra. The criterion of success is mano-laya — the mind settles into the light, does not merely picture it. (Mantras 5–6.)","Bahir-lakṣya. Eyes open, fixed straight ahead. Without forcing imagery, allow colour-spaces to arise at progressive distances in front. Then raise the gaze upward; the moving light becomes continuously perceived. (Mantras 7–8.)","Madhya-lakṣya. Best at prātaḥ-kāla (the half-hour before sunrise). Discriminate the five ākāśas — citra, cid, parama, tattva, sūrya — within the conscious space. The substrate of all five is the koṭi-sūrya-prabhā. (Mantras 9–10.)","Hold all three together. With the three stabilised, śāmbhavī-mudrā becomes possible: inner attention on the light, outer gaze unmoving, no blink. (Mantra 14.)","Surrender the lakṣya. When the inner-light contemplation is steady, the contemplator is invited to recognise: that which sees the light is itself the light. This is the entry to uttara-tāraka / amanaska. No technique compels this; it arrives. (Mantras 12–13, 15.)","Phala-śruti as integration. Reciting mantra 19 daily is itself a portion of the practice — not because of merit-accumulation, but because the explicit declaration of the path’s completeness re-grounds the practitioner."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.3  A Daily Protocol (Concrete)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A practical sequence derived from the Upaniṣad. Total: ~60 minutes. The exact durations are illustrative — match them to your sitting capacity.","<table><tr><td>Phase</td><td>Duration</td><td>What you do</td><td>What you watch for</td></tr><tr><td>Preparation</td><td>5 min</td><td>Settle posture; three rounds of slow breath; recall mantra 2 (yogi = settled mind).</td><td>Body still; restlessness named without acting on it.</td></tr><tr><td>Antar-lakṣya</td><td>15 min</td><td>Contemplate suṣumnā → heart-jyoti → ājñā-nīla-jyoti → brahma-randhra in sequence.</td><td>The light appears not as imagination but as present; mind settles into it (mano-laya).</td></tr><tr><td>Bahir-lakṣya</td><td>10 min</td><td>Eyes open; gaze fixed forward then upward; observe arising colour-spaces.</td><td>Light becomes anudṛṣṭa (continuously perceived), not flashing.</td></tr><tr><td>Madhya-lakṣya</td><td>10 min</td><td>Recognise the cid-ākāśa within which both previous lakṣyas appeared.</td><td>A radiance of \"ten million suns\" — felt as substrate, not addition.</td></tr><tr><td>Śāmbhavī</td><td>10 min</td><td>Hold all three: inner on light, outer fixed, no blink.</td><td>No straining; if the eyes burn, blink and resume.</td></tr><tr><td>Settling</td><td>5 min</td><td>Release effort. Sit without practice.</td><td>Whatever remains present; do not name it.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 19</td><td>5 min</td><td>Slow recitation, attentive to each phrase.</td><td>The practice is complete in itself today.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.4  Observations and Cautions from the Text","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The text gives no breath discipline (prāṇāyāma) as a precondition. Breath is implied as steady, not as a technique to be performed. If you have a haṭha-yoga background, do not import elaborate prāṇāyāma into Advaya-tāraka practice; the Upaniṣad chooses the simpler path.","The lights are seen, not visualised. The Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin distinguishes darśana (seeing) from kalpanā (constructing). Imagined light is the work of citra-ākāśa, the most superficial of the five ākāśas; it is not the goal.","Mano-laya, not mano-nāśa. The mind settling-into-the-light (laya) is the criterion of pūrva-tāraka. Its destruction (nāśa) is not asked for and not, in fact, attainable; what is asked is its dissolving into its source.","Uttara-tāraka cannot be willed. The text is clear: amanaska is not a technique with its own mudrā. It is the natural rest of the mind that has surrendered the last lakṣya. Effort here delays it.","Śāmbhavī-mudrā without antar-lakṣya is not the mudrā. Many manuals describe the posture (open eyes, unblinking gaze) and call that śāmbhavī. The Upaniṣad is precise: the mudrā is the combination — outer stance and inner stance held together. The outer alone is just a stare.","The guru as principle precedes the guru as person. Mantra 16’s etymological reading (gu = darkness, ru = remover) defines guru as a function — andhakāra-nirodhitva. A human teacher who serves this function is guru; one who does not, however titled, is not.","Phala-śruti is not magical compensation. The fruits listed in mantra 19 belong to one who studies the Upaniṣad nityam (daily). The implicit point: only sustained engagement yields the result. A single reading yields a fraction; the daily reading yields the whole."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"V.5  Common Errors the Text Implicitly Names","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Stopping at antar-lakṣya. The internal contemplation is the entrance, not the goal. Yogins who fix on the inner light and never move to bahir or madhya remain in a partial accomplishment.","Stopping at bahir-lakṣya. The colour-spaces in front of the nose are striking and easily mistaken for the goal. They are signposts.","Treating the five ākāśas as five objects. They are five depths of one space. Counting them as distinct loci of attention turns the practice into mental cartography.","Performing śāmbhavī without preparation. The mudrā presupposes antar-lakṣya has been stabilised. Otherwise the unblinking gaze is empty.","Imagining the koṭi-sūrya-prabhā instead of waiting for it. The radiance arrives when the discriminations have settled; until then it is merely an image.","Treating uttara-tāraka as a posture. It is not. There is no mudrā for amanaska. If you find yourself trying to \"do\" amanaska, you are still in pūrva.","Treating the phala-śruti as license. The Upaniṣad does not say recitation substitutes for practice; it says recitation by one who studies daily has these effects. The daily study is the practice."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Part VI · Major Commentaries and Critical Editions","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>A frank reading — Unlike the Sāṃkhyakārikā, the Bhagavad-Gītā, or the principal Upaniṣads (which attract a dense forest of classical Sanskrit commentaries), the Advaya-Tāraka Upaniṣad has only ONE substantial classical Sanskrit commentary in the historical record — that of Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin. The other entries below are the most consequential modern critical editions, translations, and expositions, listed in the order most useful for a serious reader.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"VI.1  Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin · Dīpikā  (classical, c. 1750 CE)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Field</td><td>Detail</td></tr><tr><td>Author</td><td>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin (also spelt Upaniṣad-Brahmendra-Yogin) — an eighteenth-century scholar-ascetic of the Kāñcī tradition; he authored Sanskrit Dīpikā commentaries on all 108 Muktikā Upaniṣads.</td></tr><tr><td>Date</td><td>c. 1750 CE (working in the mid-18th century).</td></tr><tr><td>Style</td><td>Sanskrit prose; advaitic in framework; engages each phrase of the Upaniṣad; cross-references the other Yoga Upaniṣads, the Yoga-sūtra-bhāṣya, and Śaṅkara’s commentaries.</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>For Advaya-Tāraka, compact — appropriate to the short root-text. Roughly 8–10 printed pages in the Adyar edition.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinctive readings</td><td>· Identifies the koṭi-sūrya-prabhā of mantra 10 with the Self-luminous awareness, not a metaphorical sun.<br>· Reads the three lakṣyas as three stances of one antaḥkaraṇa rather than three physical loci.<br>· Treats the guru-section (16–18) as a structural climax of the text, not an appended praise.<br>· Carefully distinguishes mano-laya (asked for here) from mano-nāśa (asked for in higher Yoga texts).</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>The only line-by-line classical Sanskrit reading. Anyone serious about the text needs this commentary — it is the baseline against which modern readings should be checked.</td></tr><tr><td>Where to find it</td><td>Adyar Library’s edition of the 108 Upaniṣads with Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s commentary (see References).</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"VI.2  K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar · English translation (1914)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Field</td><td>Detail</td></tr><tr><td>Author</td><td>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar — Theosophical Society scholar, careful translator of the Yoga Upaniṣads.</td></tr><tr><td>Edition</td><td>Thirty Minor Upanishads. Madras: Vasanta Press / Theosophical Publishing House, 1914.</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>English translation with brief annotations. Conservative; uses Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin as the principal guide.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinctive features</td><td>· One of the first complete English renderings of the 20 Yoga Upaniṣads in a single volume.<br>· Footnotes flag where Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s reading is being followed and where the translator departs.<br>· Style now archaic; still consulted because of the editorial accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>A first English approach paired with the Sanskrit. The footnotes function as a secondary commentary.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"VI.3  T. R. Śrīnivāsa Ayyaṅgār · Adyar translation (1938)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Field</td><td>Detail</td></tr><tr><td>Author</td><td>T. R. Śrīnivāsa Ayyaṅgār (with G. Srinivasa Murti).</td></tr><tr><td>Edition</td><td>The Yoga Upaniṣad-s, translated into English on the basis of the commentary of Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin. Adyar: Adyar Library, 1938 (later reprintings).</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>The most exact modern English rendering of the 20 Yoga Upaniṣads. Translates verbatim from the Adyar Sanskrit text, with the Brahma-Yogin Dīpikā as its commentary base.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinctive features</td><td>· Reliable English equivalents for the technical vocabulary (lakṣya, jyotis, tāraka, amanaska, unmanī).<br>· Apparatus identifies variant readings across manuscripts.<br>· Companion volume in Sanskrit (with Brahma-Yogin’s commentary) was issued by Adyar Library; the two together form the standard scholarly set.</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>The English reading of record. If you want to know what the Sanskrit is doing line by line, this is the translation that tracks it most faithfully.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"VI.4  Paul Deussen · Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda (1897)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Field</td><td>Detail</td></tr><tr><td>Author</td><td>Paul Deussen — German indologist; student of Schopenhauer’s reading of Indian philosophy.</td></tr><tr><td>Edition</td><td>Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda. Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1897. (English tr. by V. M. Bedekar, Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, Motilal Banarsidass, 1980.)</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>German translation with extensive philological introduction and critical apparatus. Treats Advaya-tāraka as a representative minor Upaniṣad of the Yoga group.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinctive features</td><td>· The first Western critical engagement with this Upaniṣad.<br>· Treats the text comparatively — placing it among the other Yoga Upaniṣads and noting parallels with Western mystical literature.<br>· Brief; not line-by-line; but situates the Upaniṣad in the broader history of Indian philosophy with unmatched perspective.</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Historical placement and comparative context. Less useful for the sādhaka than for the scholar.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"VI.5  A. G. Krishna Warrier · Yoga Upaniṣads (1989)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Field</td><td>Detail</td></tr><tr><td>Author</td><td>A. G. Krishna Warrier — Indian Sanskritist and theologian, Adyar Library / Kuppuswami Sastri Research Institute.</td></tr><tr><td>Edition</td><td>The Yoga Upaniṣads. Madras: Theosophical Publishing House / Adyar Library, 1989 (revising the 1914 and 1938 work).</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Modern English translation of all 20 Yoga Upaniṣads, prepared with twentieth-century philological discipline.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinctive features</td><td>· Most accessible modern English idiom while preserving technical accuracy.<br>· Brief introductions to each Upaniṣad orient the reader to its specific concerns.<br>· Useful annotations on points where Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s reading is the only classical witness.</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>A modern reader who wants the standard text in flowing English without sacrificing precision.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"VI.6  Comparative Synopsis","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Feature</td><td>Brahma-Yogin</td><td>Aiyar (1914)</td><td>Ayyaṅgār (1938)</td><td>Deussen (1897)</td><td>Warrier (1989)</td></tr><tr><td>Language</td><td>Sanskrit</td><td>English</td><td>English</td><td>German</td><td>English</td></tr><tr><td>Line-by-line</td><td>✓✓</td><td>✓</td><td>✓✓</td><td>—</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Includes Sanskrit text</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>some</td></tr><tr><td>Uses Brahma-Yogin</td><td>(is)</td><td>✓</td><td>✓✓</td><td>limited</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Variant readings noted</td><td>limited</td><td>—</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>some</td></tr><tr><td>Modern idiom</td><td>—</td><td>archaic</td><td>—</td><td>period German</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Comparative context</td><td>—</td><td>minimal</td><td>minimal</td><td>✓✓</td><td>some</td></tr><tr><td>Suited for sādhana</td><td>✓✓</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>✓</td></tr></table>","<table><tr><td>Reading order — (1) Read the Sanskrit text with Ayyaṅgār’s English to grasp the surface. (2) Read Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā for the line-by-line classical reading (in Sanskrit, with a help-text if necessary). (3) Consult Aiyar where Ayyaṅgār’s rendering puzzles you. (4) Use Deussen for historical placement, Warrier for modern idiom. The actual practice begins only after step 2.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"VI.7  Cognate Texts That Function as Implicit Commentary","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Because no other classical Sanskrit commentary on this Upaniṣad survives, the most useful \"indirect commentary\" is the cluster of cognate Yoga Upaniṣads and yoga manuals that quote or develop its themes.","<table><tr><td>Cognate text</td><td>How it illuminates Advaya-Tāraka</td></tr><tr><td>Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad</td><td>Develops the five-ākāśa scheme of mantra 9 in much greater detail. Effectively the classical \"commentary\" on madhya-lakṣya.</td></tr><tr><td>Tejo-bindu Upaniṣad</td><td>Pure-consciousness contemplation in advaitic frame; closest in tone to the uttara-tāraka portion (mantras 12–15).</td></tr><tr><td>Yoga-kuṇḍalinī Upaniṣad</td><td>Detailed account of suṣumnā and kuṇḍalinī from mantra 5; effectively a commentary on antar-lakṣya.</td></tr><tr><td>Maṇḍūkya Upaniṣad (with Gauḍapāda-kārikā)</td><td>For the doctrinal frame of advaya / non-duality presupposed in the title.</td></tr><tr><td>Haṭha-yoga-pradīpikā 4</td><td>Quotes mantra 14 verbatim on śāmbhavī; develops the mudrā as a free-standing practice.</td></tr><tr><td>Gheraṇḍa-saṃhitā 3</td><td>Treats śāmbhavī and khecarī in the haṭha-yoga frame; useful contrast to the Advaya-tāraka reading.</td></tr><tr><td>Yoga-sūtra 1.36 (\"viśokā vā jyotismatī\")</td><td>The Pātañjala phrase \"endowed with light, sorrowless\" is the closest sūtra-text parallel to pūrva-tāraka.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"References","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Primary text — Sanskrit","Yoga Upaniṣad-s, with the commentary of Śrī Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin. Adyar Library Sanskrit edition (Adyar: Adyar Library and Research Centre, 1920; reprinted multiple times). — Standard Sanskrit text of all 20 Yoga Upaniṣads with the only classical Sanskrit commentary on each.","108 Upaniṣads. Pandit Pustakalaya (Calcutta) and Nirṇaya Sāgara (Bombay) editions of the Muktikā canon contain the Advaya-Tāraka in slightly varying versifications.","Translations cited","Aiyar, K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi (tr.). Thirty Minor Upanishads. Madras: Vasanta Press / Theosophical Publishing House, 1914.","Ayyaṅgār, T. R. Śrīnivāsa &amp; Murti, G. Srinivasa (tr.). The Yoga Upaniṣad-s. Adyar: Adyar Library, 1938 (subsequent reprintings).","Deussen, Paul. Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda. Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1897. English: Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, tr. V. M. Bedekar &amp; G. B. Palsule. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1980.","Warrier, A. G. Krishna (tr.). The Yoga Upaniṣads. Madras: Theosophical Publishing House / Adyar Library, 1989.","Cognate texts (referenced in this companion)","Maṇḍala-brāhmaṇa Upaniṣad — develops the five-ākāśa scheme; in any of the above collections of the 20 Yoga Upaniṣads.","Tejo-bindu Upaniṣad — closest in tone to uttara-tāraka.","Yoga-kuṇḍalinī Upaniṣad — fuller treatment of suṣumnā / kuṇḍalinī.","Maṇḍūkya Upaniṣad with Gauḍapāda-kārikā — frame text for \"advaya\".","Haṭha-yoga-pradīpikā of Svātmārāma — chapter 4 quotes mantra 14 on śāmbhavī; edition by Brahmananda (Adyar), or critical edition by Swami Digambarji &amp; Pt. Raghunatha Shastri Kokaje (Kaivalyadhama, Lonavla, 1970).","Gheraṇḍa-saṃhitā — develops śāmbhavī and khecarī within haṭha-yoga framework.","Yoga-sūtra of Patañjali with Vyāsa-bhāṣya — for jyotismatī (1.36) and the broader context of dhyāna.","Secondary scholarship","Larson, Gerald J. &amp; Bhattacharya, Ram Shankar (eds.). Yoga: India’s Philosophy of Meditation. Encyclopedia of Indian Philosophies, vol. XII. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 2008. — Survey volume with sections on the Yoga Upaniṣads.","Mallinson, James &amp; Singleton, Mark. Roots of Yoga. London: Penguin Classics, 2017. — Modern critical reader of yoga sources with translations of select Yoga Upaniṣad passages.","Bouy, Christian. Les Nātha-Yogin et les Upaniṣads. Paris: De Boccard, 1994. — Detailed study of the relationship between the Yoga Upaniṣads and the Nātha lineage; useful historical placement of Advaya-tāraka.","Birch, Jason. \"The Amanaska: King of All Yogas. A Critical Edition and Annotated Translation.\" Doctoral dissertation, University of Oxford, 2013. — For the wider literature on amanaska/unmanī state invoked in mantras 12–15."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Closing Note on Use","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This companion presents the entire Advaya-Tāraka Upaniṣad — all 19 mantras — in Devanāgarī and IAST with literal word-by-word, accurate English translation, and a synthesis of the only surviving classical Sanskrit commentary (Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Dīpikā). The visual schemata, practical protocols, and observations are derivations from the text itself; nothing is invented.","What this companion does NOT do: it does not reproduce Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin’s Sanskrit Dīpikā verbatim. For that, the Adyar edition remains indispensable. The commentary-synthesis here is in English, drawn from the Dīpikā’s structure and principal readings; it is a working presentation, not a translation of every phrase.","Pair this document with: the Sanskrit text (Adyar); Ayyaṅgār’s English (Adyar 1938); and where possible, sustained engagement with a teacher. The Upaniṣad expressly reserves its content for one who has approached a guru (mantra 1)."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
